# Ollama — one native daemon shared by every AI environment in this repo.
# Copy to .env (deploy.sh does this for you) and edit as needed.

# Where `ollama serve` LISTENS. Leave unset for Ollama's own default, which is
# 127.0.0.1:11434 — loopback only.
#
# Set this to 0.0.0.0:11434 if anything in a CONTAINER needs to reach Ollama:
# nanoclaw-mnemon's mnemon embeddings, its Ollama MCP tool, chat-frontends,
# llm-gateways. A container reaching host.docker.internal:11434 arrives at
# this host as external traffic, and a loopback listener refuses it — which
# surfaces as `ollama_available: false` and as agent-side Ollama tools failing,
# with nothing failing on the host side to point at the cause.
#
# Trade-off, which is why this is opt-in rather than the default: binding
# 0.0.0.0 also exposes Ollama to your local network, with no authentication —
# anyone who can reach this machine on port 11434 can list, pull, delete and
# run models. On a laptop behind a firewall that is usually acceptable; on an
# open network it is not. Prefer a specific interface address over 0.0.0.0 if
# you know which one your container runtime uses.
#
# Format: host:port, no scheme. NOT a URL — this is a bind address, and
# `ollama serve` reads it from OLLAMA_HOST, which is a different meaning of
# the same variable name than OLLAMA_HOST below.
#OLLAMA_SERVE_HOST=0.0.0.0:11434

# What this repo's scripts PROBE to check Ollama is alive. A full URL, and
# almost never needs changing — it stays localhost even when OLLAMA_SERVE_HOST
# opens the daemon up, because these checks run on this host.
# Format: a URL including scheme.
#OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11434

# The `ollama` executable to invoke, if it isn't on PATH under that name.
# Format: a command name or absolute path.
#OLLAMA_CMD=ollama

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime tuning — how much RAM the shared daemon holds onto.
#
# One daemon serves every AI environment here at once (mnemon embeddings, a
# chat frontend, a gateway, direct API clients), on hosts as small as a 4GB Pi.
# Ollama's own defaults assume a machine with room to spare, so the defaults
# that matter are worth setting deliberately rather than inheriting.
#
# These are applied on the NEXT DAEMON START, by whichever mechanism this host
# uses (launchd session on macOS, a systemd drop-in on Linux, the environment
# directly for a bare `ollama serve`). A FAST deploy that finds Ollama already
# healthy leaves it running and says so — use STOP then FAST, or CLEAN, to
# restart it with changed values. Unlike the bind address, there is no way to
# read back what a running daemon started with, so nothing can detect drift
# for you.

# How long an idle model stays resident in RAM. Ollama's default is 5m.
# On a small host that is the setting that decides whether the NEXT consumer
# finds memory free: five idle minutes of a 2.5GB model pinned on a 4GB Pi is
# the difference between the next request working and the host swapping.
# Suggested: 30s-2m on a 4-8GB Pi, the 5m default on a 16GB+ Mac.
# Accepts a Go duration (30s, 5m, 1h), 0 to unload immediately, or -1 to keep
# a model loaded forever.
#OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=2m

# How many DIFFERENT models may be resident at once. Ollama's default scales
# with detected VRAM/RAM. Several consumers each wanting their own model is the
# normal case in this repo, not the exceptional one — and on a small host the
# honest answer is 1, so a second model evicts the first instead of both
# fighting for RAM that does not exist.
# Suggested: 1 on a 4-8GB host; leave unset on 16GB+.
#OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS=1

# How many requests may run concurrently against ONE model. Each concurrent
# slot costs its own KV cache on top of the shared weights, so this multiplies
# the context cost rather than the weight cost.
# Suggested: 1 on a 4-8GB host; leave unset on 16GB+.
#OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL=1
